_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 23
---
order: 23
---
short_label: VI.23
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: Equiangular parallelograms have to one another the ratio compounded of the ratios of their sides.
----
proof:

Let {AC} and {CF} be equiangular parallelograms having the angle {BCD|angBCD} equal to the angle {ECG|angECG}.

I say that the parallelogram {AC} has to the parallelogram {CF} the ratio compounded of the ratios of the sides.

Let them be placed so that {BC} is in a straight line with {CG}.  Then {DC} is also in a straight line with {CE}. [!just I.14]

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="380" height="270" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="VI.23 diagram" src="propVI23.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's figure draws the parallelograms ABCD and FECG (named AC and
    // CF by their diagonals) placed with BCG and DCE straight through C,
    // the completing parallelogram DCGH (his DG, also called CH), and the
    // three lines K, L, M (slider ends on long invisible tracks), L and M
    // fixed by proportion points.
    //
    // DCGH and H stay off the slides until "Complete the parallelogram DG";
    // its two new sides are solid slide twins DH and GH, hidden at rest
    // (initiallyHidden), stroking from D and G. K, L, M arrive as they are
    // set out. Everything else added is transparent: the faceless
    // parallelograms AC, CF, CH (explicit ;0 faces — a highlighted filled
    // polygon is raised over the figure, #140) and the sides BC, CG, DC, CE.
    // The equal angles BCD and ECG share a colour and are pinned to one
